%S A402292 1,2,3,5,7,9,11,14,16,18,21,23,26,28,31,33,36,38,41,44,46,49,51,54,57,
%T A402292 59,62,65,67,70,73,75,78,81,83,86,89,92,94,97,100,102,105,108,111,113,
%U A402292 116,119,122,124,127,130,133,135,138,141,144,146,149,152,155,158,160,163,166,169,171,174,177,180,183,185,188,191,194,197,199
%N A402292 Maximum number of regions that can be formed in the plane by drawing n noncrossing unit circles.
%C A402292 The circles may touch, but may not cross each other. Since they have equal radii, touching circles must be externally tangent.
%C A402292 It will appear (see FORMULA) that this sequence is equal to A047932 + 2, but the subject is of sufficiant interest to justify its own entry. It is also a companion to A402293.
%H A402292 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A402292/b402292.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%H A402292 David O. H. Cutler, Jonas Karlsson, and Neil J. A. Sloane, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.15864">Cutting a Pancake with an Exotic Knife</a>, arXiv:2511.15864[math.CO], v3, April 19 2026. Also <a href="https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL29/Sloane/sloane8.pdf">Journal of Integer Sequences</a>, Vol. 29 (#26.4.5), 2026.
%H A402292 Scott R. Shannon, <a href="/A402292/a402292.jpg">Image of a circle arrangement for a(23) = 54</a>.
%F A402292 Theorem: a(n) = floor(3*n-sqrt(12*n-3)) + 2, for n >= 1.
%F A402292 Sketch of proof: We may represent the circles by an arrangement of n pennies on a tabletop. To maximize the number of regions, we push the pennies together as tightly as possible, although of course this can be done in many ways. The "penny graph" (see A085632, A085633) has a vertex for each penny and an edge whenever two pennies touch.
%F A402292 Assuming (as we may) that this graph is connected, the number of regions R is n plus the number (C) of internal regions in the graph + 1 (for the exterior region).  By Euler's formula, C = E - n + 1, where E is the number of edges in the graph. So R = E + 2. From A047932, we know the maximum value of E is floor(3*n-sqrt(12*n-3)). QED
%F A402292 As pointed out in A047932, an optimal arrangement of the pennies is to place them along a hexagonal spiral (cf. A056105).
%t A402292 A402292[n_] := If[n == 0, 1, Floor[3*n - Sqrt[12*n - 3]] + 2];
%t A402292 Array[A402292, 100, 0] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Nov 20 2026 *)
%Y A402292 Cf. A000124, A047932, A056105, A386480, A402293.
%K A402292 nonn,easy,new
%O A402292 0,2
%A A402292 _Jonas Karlsson_ and _N. J. A. Sloane_, Nov 20 2026
%E A402292 a(0) = 1 corrected by _Paolo Xausa_, Nov 20 2026
